🌊 What is PowerShell (in simple words)?

Think of PowerShell as a supercharged version of Command Prompt (CMD).

CMD = basic commands (simple, text only)
PowerShell = advanced + smarter + more powerful

👉 It helps you:

Control your computer
Automate tasks (do things automatically)
Write scripts (like small programs)
🧠 The Big Idea (MOST IMPORTANT)

👉 PowerShell works with "objects" instead of plain text

What does that even mean?
🔹 CMD (old way):
Gives output as plain text

Example:

dir

Output = just text (you can't easily manipulate it)

🔹 PowerShell (new way):
Gives output as objects (data with structure)

Think of an object like this:

File:
  Name = notes.txt
  Size = 2KB
  Type = Text File

👉 This means:

You can filter it
Sort it
Modify it easily

💡 That’s why it's called:
👉 Object-Oriented

🧩 Answer to your question

What do we call the advanced approach used to develop PowerShell?

👉 Object-Oriented

🧰 What is PowerShell made of?

PowerShell is actually 3 things combined:

🖥️ Command-line tool (like CMD)
📜 Scripting language (you can write scripts)
⚙️ Automation tool (do repetitive tasks automatically)
🧱 PowerShell Commands (called cmdlets)

PowerShell commands follow a pattern:

👉 Verb-Noun

Examples:

Command	Meaning
Get-Content	Read a file
Set-Location	Change folder
Get-Command	Show all commands

💡 Easy trick:

Get = read
Set = change
New = create
Remove = delete
🔄 CMD vs PowerShell (simple comparison)
Feature	CMD	PowerShell
Output	Text	Objects
Power	Basic	Advanced
Commands	dir, echo	Get-ChildItem, Write-Output
Automation	Limited	Very powerful
🚀 How to open PowerShell

Easy ways:

Press Windows + R, type:

powershell
Search “PowerShell” in Start Menu

Open CMD and type:

powershell
