📁 Navigating Files in PowerShell (Beginner Level)

Think of your computer like a folder system (files inside folders).

PowerShell gives you commands (cmdlets) to:

See files
Move between folders
Create/delete files
Read file content
🔑 Basic Commands (Super Important)
📂 1. View files in a folder
Get-ChildItem

👉 Same as:

dir (CMD)
ls (Linux)

💡 Meaning:

“Show me everything inside this folder”

📍 2. Move between folders
Set-Location C:\Users

👉 Same as:

cd

💡 Meaning:

“Go to this directory”

🆕 3. Create file or folder
New-Item test.txt

💡 Meaning:

“Create something new”

❌ 4. Delete file/folder
Remove-Item test.txt

💡 Meaning:

“Delete this”

📖 5. Read file content
Get-Content file.txt

👉 Same as:

type (CMD)
cat (Linux)
🧠 Your Questions Explained
❓ What replaces type?

👉 ✅ Get-Content

Because:

It reads file content (same job as type)

❓ How to show contents of C:\Users?

👉 ✅

Get-ChildItem -Path C:\Users

💡 Why?

Get-ChildItem → list files
-Path → where to look
❓ Why answer = 4 items?

👉 That’s from the lab environment

Meaning:

That specific folder had 4 items inside

⚠️ On your PC, it might be different.

🔗 Piping (MOST IMPORTANT CONCEPT)

This is where PowerShell becomes 🔥 powerful.

👉 Symbol:
|

💡 Meaning:

“Take output of first command → send it to next command”

🔍 Example:
Get-ChildItem | Where-Object Extension -eq .txt
Step-by-step:
Get-ChildItem → gets all files
| → pass them forward
Where-Object → filter them
Extension -eq .txt → only .txt files

👉 Final meaning:

“Show only .txt files”

🧠 Filtering (VERY IMPORTANT)
🔹 Where-Object

Used to filter data
